  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 320 AM MST Sun Nov 8 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nThis morning, the upper level ridge of high pressure breaks down,\nresulting in a tightening pressure gradient over Montana. This \ncreates windy conditions today, mainly across North-Central MT. \nThe strongest winds will be along the eastern slopes of the Rocky \nMountain Front, where favorable thermal conditions exist for \nmountain wave development. This, combined with diurnal heating, \nwill allow the stronger winds aloft to mix down to the surface, \ncreating the possibility of isolated 100 MPH gusts along the \nFront. The remainder of North-Central MT will see gusts closer to \n65 MPH. By this evening, the winds will have greatly dissipated, \nas the remainder of the aforementioned ridge moves east and we \ntransition to zonal flow aloft across the state. This will result\nin slightly cooler, but still seasonally average, temperatures \nMonday and Tuesday.\n\nIncreased moisture aloft will be transported along the zonal \nflow, resulting in the chance of light precipitation over the \nhigher elevations of the Northern Rockies this evening through \ntomorrow morning. Snow levels will lower down to around 4000 ft \nnorth of Lincoln, creating the potential for a light dusting of \nsnow over Marias Pass. For the rest of the CWA, this increased \nmoisture aloft will simply manifest as cloudy skies.\n\nOn Tuesday, the zonal flow aloft gets pushed eastward by a \nshortwave trough digging southward through southwest MT. This will\ncreate a tightening surface pressure gradient, producing another \nday of windy conditions across Central and North-Central MT. Once \nagain, the strongest winds will be along the Rocky Mountain Front.\nBy Wednesday, a strong upper level ridge will have built over the\nIdaho Panhandle, putting the region under its influence and \nsubsequently allowing temperatures to rise. Through the rest of \nthe forecast period, the ridge will slowly move eastward until \nMontana sits roughly along its axis, allowing for the continued \ntrend of seasonally warm temperatures and little to no chance of \nprecipitation. -Dzomba\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nSounding and cross sectional analysis was showing increased upper\nlevel winds, as well as favorable thermal conditions for mountain\nwave development along the Rocky Mountian Front. This analysis, \nalong with model guidance, was supporting upper level winds as \nstrong as 100 MPH mixing down to the surface by late Sunday \nmorning across the Front, as well as upper level winds as strong \nas 60-70 MPH mixing down to the surface across the rest of North- \nCentral MT. As such, the high wind warning for the Rocky Mountain\nFront was updated to communicate gusts around 95 MPH being \nexpected, with isolated gusts near 100 MPH being possible. \n\nWhile average 850 mb - 700 mb winds do not look as strong \nthroughout the rest of North-Central MT, they are still around 65 \nMPH, with just 700 mb winds peaking around 70 MPH. Model guidance \nalso shows strong winds spreading across the Hi-Line, with chances\nfor pockets of stronger winds along Highway 87. As such, the high\nwind watches have been upgraded to high wind warnings and have \nbeen expanded to include the rest of North-Central MT. However, \nthese high wind warnings are for lesser winds, with gusts up to 65\nMPH expected. -Dzomba",
